        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://sharpsburghistory.com/town-history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Sharpsburg was the site of the bloodiest single‑day battle in American history, with roughly 23,000 casualties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sharpsburghistory.com/town-history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The arrival of the railroad and the Chesapeake and Ohio Canal helped revitalize Sharpsburg’s local economy in the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sharpsburghistory.com/town-history",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Before the town’s founding, the area that is now Sharpsburg was primarily inhabited by Native American tribes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
